[ENG.FullName]
The Liberation of Europe
[ENG.Description]

[ENG.Epilogue]
By the beginning of 1945, the Western Front passed along the lower reaches of the Meuse River, then went north in the Arnhem area and then along the West German border to Switzerland. There were deviations from this front line only in small areas. The balance of forces on the Western Front was in favor of the allied forces of the anti-Hitler coalition. They were better armed and had a larger number, unlike the German divisions that had a significant shortage in staffing. In March, the Allied forces advanced towards the Rhine, virtually unopposed. After the encirclement of Army Group "B" in the Ruhr industrial district, the defense of the Wehrmacht troops in the central sector of the Western Front actually collapsed. Subsequently, developing an offensive into the depths of Germany, the Allied armies reached the Elbe River, advanced eastward south of Berlin and joined forces with the troops of the Soviet Army.

In Italy, the Allied forces during the offensive, together with the Italian liberation forces, forced the enemy to capitulate. On April 29, 1945, at the headquarters of the Commander-in-Chief of the Allied forces in the Mediterranean area, G. Alexander, representatives of the German command in Italy signed a document on unconditional surrender, which entered into force on May 2.

The United States entered the war shortly before its end. They undoubtedly contributed to the victory over Japan and Germany, but their wait-and-see attitude allowed them to avoid serious losses in the war. On the contrary, according to the results of the Second World War, the United States earned a lot of money, and did not lose it. Soon it was the United States that became the first economy in the world, taking the palm from the weakened Great Britain. Japan has been under American control for a long time. Soon the economic and political confrontation between the USA and the USSR began.
Great Britain, the former largest colonial power, was seriously depleted and soon ceased to be the first economy in the world, losing the primacy to the United States. She could no longer control her numerous colonies either, so soon they gained independence one by one.However, not once. Also, following the results of the Second World War, the influence of Great Britain on the world stage decreased.
France has also lost its former influence, at least partially. In addition, it granted independence to some (and then all) of its colonies, while retaining, however, some overseas territories. Austria was separated from Germany and became a sovereign state. Hungary lost part of the territory, transferring it to Czechoslovakia.

[U001.FullName]
Overlord
[U001.Description]
Allied leaders have gathered for a conference in Teheran in the late autumn of 1943. During the conference the final decision was made to open the second front by a unified British, U.S. and Canadian task force. The preparations for this operation lasted for more than six months, and Operation Overlord finally launched in June 1944. It led to the landing of 43 Allied Divisions in Normandy, and went down in history as the largest assault operation during the Second World War.

The quiet French town of Saint-Mere-Eglise, near the seashore, had been in the epicenter of conflict for years. The Germans seized it on June 18, 1940 and established a garrison in the town for the next four years. Throughout the first half of 1944, the Allies made frequent air strikes against the town. In the pre-dawn hours of June 6, the largest sea armada ever assembled landed on the Normandy shores. This was the beginning of Operation Overlord.

[U002.FullName]
Death’s Road
[U002.Description]
Days after the D-Day landings, theAllies were expanding and deepening their beachhead into Normandy. The Germans defended fiercely feeding their divisions into battle unit by unit. In the enemy rear, fighters of the French Resistance were hampering the advance of troops in every possible way. Resistance of the Nazis was extremely fierce in the areas around the city of Caen, where English and Canadian units were engaged. Early on the morning of June 13, 1944 a long column of British armored vehicles was moving along the road towards Caen. These were the famous ‘Desert Rats’ that had won glory by their successful actions in North Africa.

At the same time, the German tanker ace Michael Wittmann, the commander of the 2nd Company of the 101st SS-Panzer Abteilung received orders to stop the British 7th Armored Division advance on Caen. Three tanks in Wittmann’s company were in operating condition and two more remained in the rear for maintenance.
		   
[U003.FullName]
Avalanche
[U003.Description]
As for the campaign in Normandy, the priority for the Allies was to gain a secure lodgment with respective supply lines in the area between Cherbourg and the mouth of the Orne River. By the end of June, US units under the command of General Collins approached Cherbourg. The Germans were ready and had built up their defenses as best they could.  Cherbourg was an important target since it would afford the allies a much-needed deep-water port in Northern France.

[U004.FullName]
Saint Lo
[U004.Description]
After their failure to defend the coast, German troops concentrated their forces along the main Allied axis of advance. Both sides considered the city of Saint Lo as crucial. The surrounding terrain enabled the Germans to build up a robust defense. Operation Cobra was launched on July 25 in order to finalize the breakout from Normandy and breakthrough into the heart of France.

[U005.FullName]
Caen
[U005.Description]
One of the key objectives of British forces on D-Day was the capture of the strategically vital city of Caen. Caen was always seen as the key to Normandy and the springboard for the Allied breakout, but the Germans were also keenly aware of this and held out for over a month. After three major offensives and more than 30 bloody days of struggle, Caen finally fell, the city ruined and this crucial victory helped to pave the way for the launch of Operation Cobra later in July. 

[U006.FullName]
Luttich
[U006.Description]
The French town of Mortaine became the focus of a German counterattack late on the evening of August 6, 1944. Operation Luttich, as it was called, was ordered by Hitler with the intention of cutting off the American troops now driving through Brittany and Maine. The Germans needed control of the vital road network around Mortain to allow a drive to Avranches on the coast.

[U007.FullName]
Closing the Pocket
[U007.Description]
The success of the Allied troops landing in Normandy was the beginning of the German Army’s defeat on the Western Front. Under the combined pressure of the Americans and French to the south, the British to the west, and the Canadians and Poles to the north, the German Seventh Army, which has retreated to the area around the town of Falaise, was facing a possible encirclement.  Throughout all of August, through indecission and some inter-army bickering, the Allies were unable to complete their encirclement around the villages of Chmbois, Saint Lambert, Turn and Tournai-sur-Dives and approximately 100,000 German troops were able to escape although they left behind over 10,000 dead and more than 10,000 guns and vehicles destroyed. In addition nearly 50,000 German soldiers surrendered.

[U008.FullName]
Pursuit
[U008.Description]
The Germans are retreating throughout this sector of the front, but while their Divisions seem to be on the run, they leave behind rear guards that are constantly slowing down our advances. The enemy takes advantage of every defensive and terrain benefit they can and are own casualties are mounting and our progress is beginning to grind to a halt.  Momentum must be maintained and these defensive holdouts must be crushed.

[U010.FullName]
Bulge
[U010.Description]
The German offensive launched in December of 1944 achieved impressive initial results but their forces couldn’t dislodge the stubborn defenders of the town of Bastogne. Hampered by bad weather at the beginning of the operation, by December 23 the skies above began to clear and the full force of Allied Airpower began to strike at the exposed German troops. With the arrival of Patton’s 3rd Army, the Allies slowly and deliberately forced the German units back and recaptured the areas lost weeks before.
